#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fd7ea9-c5cf-5491-8b4d-07208bb52a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358655} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5b4e62-b0ab-5606-a50e-773260737de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358656} {:answer/id #uuid "a576bfeb-f50e-5d34-b75d-b4f735112802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358657} {:answer/id #uuid "00f0cf9c-938f-5535-8a8f-976a1870e794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358658} {:answer/id #uuid "18e14d21-b6c3-5982-a761-c3ddc12633b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c5b4e62-b0ab-5606-a50e-773260737de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358656} :answer-field/id #uuid "0307a327-bfa9-5d9e-af9a-20a228fb4cd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e4e308-8907-50e5-ac2c-e0df07b3b6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110284" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+(-3)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-5}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc808bf-2179-5e7b-a79d-c5ea3ac5270d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358662} {:answer/id #uuid "1aba46c3-a1db-57c3-a68f-5042be559f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358663} {:answer/id #uuid "af31f27e-8dd0-5ad0-b54b-9190421b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358664} {:answer/id #uuid "37445c44-a558-548e-b880-ae365bd0f358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358665} {:answer/id #uuid "d7efbf48-9657-5d03-90a0-8c5167d4dfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af31f27e-8dd0-5ad0-b54b-9190421b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358664} :answer-field/id #uuid "5861a3c0-d5a2-5dcc-b42d-0b4f16c4e9c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db615c68-58b9-59cf-a49b-080adcb0dc8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110347" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5b27cc-72ce-5757-983e-126e34951310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c5b27cc-72ce-5757-983e-126e34951310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358669} :answer-field/id #uuid "62d7696f-552c-513c-98d7-7ef24cade1db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cebaf180-0ec8-5580-ae72-a70d7fc75656" :question/math-academy-id "q-312581" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2}{x+2}$ and the point $P(-1,-5)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-5,-1)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1,-5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-2}{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1,-5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b4f9f9-3f99-563e-bee5-09b39040d4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358672} {:answer/id #uuid "294dfcb7-bdaf-5a95-8a74-13259c3185f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358673} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cc340a-eb97-5670-bf4e-8eabcde19bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358674} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8f096d-c958-504d-89c3-b044a8399e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358675} {:answer/id #uuid "14fe2aa3-6163-5eb0-930b-8f7304f87eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f8f096d-c958-504d-89c3-b044a8399e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358675} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf66df6-87fa-53e7-8d28-476241693f2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a013a9-ba3a-57f2-9361-b990abac67ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-75832" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(7).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $9$ | $-1$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ | $-4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(7)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(7)=4$ and ${g}^{′}(4)=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93df8dc9-5514-5610-9ba7-f8141c1ad15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063694} {:answer/id #uuid "aa054826-9bc5-55fa-91f8-c8a1331439de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063695} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e50e9-5afc-5ec8-9608-e1478bb62e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063696} {:answer/id #uuid "02eb72f8-e800-5b85-8c72-f9a7b308c956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063697} {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4358ed-8a57-5c21-93f2-ea7a5edec4a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dd725b3-7f44-5fca-9ff0-b43869ecbad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75986" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
